C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:271: 「南邊到西布倫，西邊到亞設，又向日出之地，達到約但河那裡的猶大」：『達到約但河那裡的猶大』拿弗他利支派的地業並未與猶大相鄰，本句意指約但河向南流直到死海，猶大地就在死海的西面；另有聖經學者認為猶大是靠近約但河岸的一個城鎮名，但無跡可尋。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:278: 〔文意註解〕「堅固的城，就是西丁、側耳、哈末、拉甲、基尼烈」：『西丁』位於伯善北方約24公里，基尼烈城南方約17公里；『側耳』確實地點不詳，似應在加利利海西面，位於西丁和哈末之間；『哈末』位於伯善北方約30公里，基尼烈城南方約11公里；『拉甲』位於伯善北方約34公里，基尼烈城南方約6公里；『基尼烈』位於伯善北方約41公里，夏瑣南方約17公里。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:280: 【書十九36】「亞大瑪、拉瑪、夏瑣、」
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:283: 〔原文字義〕「亞大瑪」地土；「拉瑪」小山丘；「夏瑣」城堡。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:285: 〔文意註解〕「亞大瑪、拉瑪、夏瑣」：『亞大瑪』位於基尼烈城西北方約14公里，夏瑣西南方約19公里；『拉瑪』不同於8節和29節的「拉瑪」，位於基尼烈城西北方約17公里，夏瑣西南方約16公里；『夏瑣』位於基尼烈城北方約17公里，亞柯東北方約45公里。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:287: 【書十九37】「基低斯、以得來、隱夏瑣、」
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:290: 〔原文字義〕「基低斯」聖所；「以得來」好的牧草地；「隱夏瑣」夏瑣(城堡)之泉。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:292: 〔文意註解〕「基低斯、以得來、隱夏瑣」：『基低斯』位於夏瑣北方約11公里，但城西南方約18公里；『以得來』不同於書十三31的「以得來」，確實地點不詳，似應在加利利地的北部，隱夏瑣附近；『隱夏瑣』位於夏瑣北方約22公里，基底斯西方約16公里。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:299: 〔文意註解〕「以利穩、密大伊勒、和璉、伯亞納、伯示麥，共十九座城，還有屬城的村莊」：『以利穩』位於夏瑣北方約16公里，基底斯西南方約13公里；『密大伊勒』確實地點不詳，似應在以利穩與和璉之間；『和璉』位於夏瑣西北西方約18公里，亞革悉東方約25公里；『伯亞納』位於夏瑣西南方約23公里，但城西南西方約21公里；『伯示麥』不同於22節和書十五10的「伯示麥」，位於夏瑣西方約20公里，亞革悉東方約22公里；『共十九座城』33~38節所列城鎮合計。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:301: 【書十九39】「這些城並屬城的村莊，就是拿弗他利支派按著宗族所得的地業。」
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:304: 〔原文字義〕「拿弗他利」摔角。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:306: 〔文意註解〕「這些城並屬城的村莊，就是拿弗他利支派按著宗族所得的地業」：請參閱32節註解。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:308: 【書十九40】「為但支派，按著宗族，拈出第七鬮。」
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:313: 〔文意註解〕「為但支派，按著宗族，拈出第七鬮」：但支派的地業位於便雅憫的西面直到地中海，南北夾在猶大和以法蓮之間，相較於其他支派，顯得很小。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:315: 【書十九41】「他們地業的境界是瑣拉、以實陶、伊珥示麥、」
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:318: 〔原文字義〕「瑣拉」大黃蜂；「以實陶」懇求；「伊珥示麥」太陽神之城。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:320: 〔文意註解〕「他們地業的境界是瑣拉、以實陶、伊珥示麥」：『瑣拉』位於耶路撒冷西方約23公里，梭烈谷中段北岸；『以實陶』位於耶路撒冷西方約22公里，基遍西南方約19公里；『伊珥示麥』位於耶路撒冷西方約24公里，希伯崙西北方約27公里。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:327: 〔文意註解〕「沙拉賓、亞雅崙、伊提拉」：『沙拉賓』位於伯特利西南方約24公里，瑣拉北方約11公里；『亞雅崙』位於基遍西方約15公里，伯示麥北方約11公里；『伊提拉』可能位於亞雅崙東方約5公里，確實地點不詳。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:329: 【書十九43】「以倫、亭拿他、以革倫、」
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:332: 〔原文字義〕「以倫」松節油，強大的；「亭拿他」一部分；「以革倫」移民，連根拔起。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:334: 〔文意註解〕「以倫、亭拿他、以革倫」：『以倫』位於耶路撒冷西北方約19公里，伯特利西南方約21公里；『亭拿他』位於耶路撒冷西方約31公里，伯示麥西北方約7公里；『以革倫』位於基色西南方約11公里，亞實突東方約18公里。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:348: 〔文意註解〕「伊胡得、比尼比拉、迦特臨門」：『伊胡得』位於約帕東方約13公里，示羅西方約37公里；『比尼比拉』位於約帕西南方約8公里的海邊；『迦特臨門』位於約帕東北方約7公里半，亞弗西方約12公里。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:357: 【書十九47】「但人的地界，越過原得的地界，因為但人上去攻取利善，用刀擊殺城中的人，得了那城，住在其中，以他們先祖但的名，將利善改名為但。」
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:362: 〔文意註解〕「但人的地界，越過原得的地界，因為但人上去攻取利善」：『越過原得的地界』指不在相鄰的地方擴充地業，而是到以色列人尚未征服的土地去取得；『利善』是但城的原名，位於加利利海北方約40公里，夏瑣北北東方約27公里。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:364: 「用刀擊殺城中的人，得了那城，住在其中，以他們先祖但的名，將利善改名為但」：本節是約書亞死後所發生的事，故是後人的補遺。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:366: 〔話中之光〕(一)但支派後來沒有順服神的安排去攻取分給他們的沿海平原，卻避難就易，「越過原得的地界」，到北方的黑門山下攻取了神沒有給他們的「利善」。但支派不服神的權柄，叫雅各在預言中不能不「等候神的救恩」(創四十九18)。大災難中十二支派的名單用利未代替了但的名字(參啟七4~8)，可能因為「假基督」就是從但支派出來的。然而先知以西結看到將來國度顯現的時候，但支派還是在國度中有分(參結四十八1)，雖然人總是不停地悖逆，但神仍然用信實和憐憫紀念祂所選召的亞伯拉罕的後裔。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:368: (二)但支派的人覺得所分的地業有些地方不易攻取，所以避難就易，攻取利善，他們知道在那裡較易取勝。在事情容易時，人人皆會信靠神，但在環境看似不利時，就是對我們的信心與勇氣的考驗了。要信靠神，祂的大能足可解決最困難的情勢。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:370: 【書十九48】「這些城並屬城的村莊，就是但支派按著宗族所得的地業。」
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:375: 〔文意註解〕「這些城並屬城的村莊，就是但支派按著宗族所得的地業」：請參閱40節註解。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:377: 【書十九49】「以色列人按著境界分完了地業，就在他們中間將地給嫩的兒子約書亞為業，」
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:380: 〔原文字義〕「地業(原文雙字)」土地(首字)；財產，產業(次字)；「中間」中間，當中；「嫩」魚，後裔；「約書亞」耶和華是拯救。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:382: 〔文意註解〕「以色列人按著境界分完了地業，就在他們中間將地給嫩的兒子約書亞為業」：約書亞的家族一直到以色列人分配完產業之地，仍未有確定的地方可供他們定居下來，這種大公無私的心態，是後世信徒領袖的好榜樣。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:384: 〔話中之光〕(一)約書亞是最後分得土地的。從這個程式中我們可以看出這位領袖人格的高尚。他沒有利用職務的便利以權謀私。他抵制了領袖們經常遭遇的試探，即犧牲弱勢群體來中飽私囊。約書亞雖然在百姓中德高望重，卻把自己的事放在最後。他把民族的利益放在個人的私利之上。約書亞為一切擔任國家或教會公職的人樹立了榜樣。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:386: (二)聖經還提到約書亞未經百姓的同意，絕不將土地占為己有。聖經說以色列人把地給了約書亞。他們愛戴自己的領袖。怪不得在約書亞和與他同工的長老有生之年，以色列人都專心侍奉耶和華(參書二十四31；士二7)。這種無私的服務會引發愛心，反過來會激勵人順從。沒有學會無私服務的人無權擔任領導職位。不求自己的喜悅(羅十五3)的基督，乃是無私服務的偉大榜樣。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:388: (三)「就在他們中間將地給嫩的兒子約書亞為業。」約書亞預表基督，他將地業置於弟兄中間。主耶穌說：「因為無論在那裡，有兩三個人奉我的名聚會，那裡就有我在他們中間」(太十八20)。祂是一切聯合的中心。我們來自不同的地方，有不同的想法與觀念，彼此沒有什麼特別的關係。但是經祂的觸摸，我們都連結起來。我們相交的中心不是講章或主餐甚至禮拜的形式，而是基督本身。讓祂成為你家庭生活的中心，職業生活的中心，甚至在任何的情況之下。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:390: 【書十九50】「是照耶和華的吩咐，將約書亞所求的城，就是以法蓮山地的亭拿西拉城，給了他。他就修那城，住在其中。」
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:393: 〔原文字義〕「以法蓮」我將獲雙倍果子；兩堆灰燼；「亭拿西拉」太陽的一部分，豐盛的一部分。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:395: 〔文意註解〕「是照耶和華的吩咐，將約書亞所求的城，就是以法蓮山地的亭拿西拉城，給了他」：『約書亞所求的城』注意他不是求好的城，而是求別的家族沒有看中的城；『亭拿西拉』位於伯特利西北方約15公里，示羅西南方約19公里。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:399: 【書十九51】「這就是祭司以利亞撒和嫩的兒子約書亞，並以色列各支派的族長，在示羅會幕門口，耶和華面前，拈鬮所分的地業。這樣，他們把地分完了。」
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:402: 〔原文字義〕「以利亞撒」神已幫助；「示羅」安歇之地。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:404: 〔文意註解〕「這就是祭司以利亞撒和嫩的兒子約書亞，並以色列各支派的族長」：『各支派的族長』指九支派半的首領，加上以利亞撒和約書亞，共有十二個人。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:406: 「在示羅會幕門口，耶和華面前，拈鬮所分的地業。這樣，他們把地分完了」：分配地業的事宜是在示羅進行的(參書十八1)。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:414: 一、西緬支派地業的境界和城邑(1~9節)：
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:416: 1.「為西緬支派的人，按著宗族，拈出第二鬮。他們所得的地業是在猶大人地業中間。…因為猶大人的份過多」(1，9節)：西緬支派的地業是在猶大人地業中間。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:420: 二、西布倫支派地業的境界和城邑(10~16節)：
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:422: 1.「為西布倫人，按著宗族，拈出第三鬮。他們地業的境界是到撒立。…達到約念前的河。…又從撒立往東轉向日出之地，…通到伊弗他伊勒谷」(11~14節)：西布倫支派的地業是被以薩迦、亞設、拿弗他利三支派包圍。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:426: 三、以薩迦支派地業的境界和城邑(17~23節)：
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:428: 1.「為以薩迦人，按著宗族，拈出第四鬮。他們的境界是到耶斯列…書念…拉璧、基善」(17~21節)：以薩迦支派的地業是被瑪拿西、拿弗他利、西布倫三支派包圍。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:432: 四、亞設支派地業的境界和城邑(24~31節)：
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:434: 1.「為亞設支派，按著宗族，拈出第五鬮。「他們的境界是黑甲…往西達到迦密…轉向日出之地…直到西頓大城」(24~28節)：亞設支派的地業是在瑪拿西支派的北面，西布倫和拿弗他利兩支派的西面。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:438: 五、拿弗他利支派地業的境界和城邑(32~39節)：
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:440: 1「為拿弗他利人，按著宗族，拈出第六鬮。他們的境界是從希利弗…其終點是約但河…南邊到西布倫，西邊到亞設」(32~34節)：拿弗他利支派的地業是在以薩迦支派的北面，西布倫和亞設兩支派的東面，以約但河為東界。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:442: 2「堅固的城，就是…基尼烈…拉瑪、夏瑣…伯示麥，共十九座城」(35~39節)：共有十九座城。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:444: 六、但支派地業的境界和城邑(40~48節)：
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:446: 1.「為但支派，按著宗族，拈出第七鬮。他們地業的境界是瑣拉、以實陶…以革倫…並約帕對面的地界」(40~46節)：但支派的地業是在猶大支派的北面，便雅憫和以法蓮的西面，瑪拿西支派的南面，以地中海為西界。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:448: 2.「但人的地界，越過原得的地界，因為但人上去攻取利善，…將利善改名為但」(47~48節)：但支派另外攻取拿弗他利支派地業東北方的利善(拉億)。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:450: 七、約書亞得以法蓮山地的亭拿西拉城(49~51節)：
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:452: 1.「以色列人…在他們中間將地給嫩的兒子約書亞為業，是照耶和華的吩咐，將約書亞所求的城，就是以法蓮山地的亭拿西拉城，給了他」(49~50節)：約書亞得以法蓮山地的亭拿西拉城為業。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:454: 2.「這就是祭司以利亞撒和嫩的兒子約書亞，並以色列各支派的族長，在示羅會幕門口，耶和華面前，拈鬮所分的地業」(51節)：以利亞撒和約書亞在示羅會幕門口，耶和華面前，拈鬮分配地業。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:457: 黃迦勒《基督徒文摘解經系列──約書亞記註解》
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_CT_joshua_19.txt:459: 參考書目：請參閱「約書亞記提要」末尾處
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\ccbiblestudy_GT_joshua_19.txt:450: ●「約旦河那裏的猶大」：Judah, the Jordan。七十士譯本(LXX) 缺「那裏的猶大」。可能在約旦河（the Jordan）北端有一個名叫猶大的城鎮（不同於猶大支派）。
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:1: Simeon’s Inheritance
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:3: Joshua 19:1
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:5: The second lot came out for the clans of the tribe of Simeon:
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:6: Israel’s inheritance was apportioned by lot before the LORD, acknowledging His authority over the land He had promised to Abraham’s descendants. Simeon, Jacob’s second son by Leah, received its portion according to its clans, preserving both tribal identity and family inheritance.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:8: Num 26:12-14 These were the descendants of Simeon by their clans:…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:10: Jos 18:10 And Joshua cast lots for them in the presence of the LORD…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:12: Gen 49:5-7 Simeon and Levi are brothers; their swords are weapons…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:14: Their inheritance lay within the territory of Judah
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:15: Simeon’s cities were assigned from Judah’s extensive southern territory, since Judah’s allotment proved larger than its population required (Joshua 19:9). The arrangement fulfilled Jacob’s prophecy that Simeon would be scattered in Israel (Genesis 49:7), yet it also provided the tribe with a secure place among God’s people. Simeon remained connected to Judah throughout Israel’s history and shared in the life and defense of the southern kingdom.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:17: Jdg 1:3 Then the men of Judah said to their brothers the Simeonites…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:19: Jdg 1:17 Then the men of Judah went with their brothers…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:23: Joshua 19:2
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:26: Beersheba stood at Israel’s southern extremity and remained closely linked with Abraham and Isaac, who worshiped the Lord there and dealt with Abimelech (Genesis 21:31–33; 26:23–25). Its inclusion in Simeon’s inheritance placed the tribe within land long associated with God’s covenant faithfulness. “Sheba” may designate the same settlement or a related locality.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:35: Moladah lay in the Negev, among the towns assigned to Simeon within Judah’s larger territory. Simeon’s scattered inheritance fulfilled Jacob’s earlier prophecy while preserving the tribe’s place among God’s people (Genesis 49:7).
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:43: Joshua 19:3
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:46: A settlement in the Negev, Hazar-shual, meaning “village of the fox,” assigned to Simeon from Judah’s extensive territory. Its later appearance in Nehemiah 11:27 shows that the region remained inhabited after the exile, preserving Israel’s connection to the land God had given.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:55: Also called Bilhah in 1 Chronicles 4:29, Balah belonged to Simeon’s scattered towns within southern Judah. Simeon’s inheritance fulfilled God’s provision for every tribe while binding Simeon’s life and security closely to Judah.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:59: 2Sa 6:2 And he and all his troops set out for Baale of Judah…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:61: 1Ch 13:6 David and all Israel went up to Baalah of Judah…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:70: Joshua 19:4
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:73: Eltolad, meaning “God’s kindred” or “God’s generation,” lay among the towns assigned to Simeon within Judah’s southern territory. Simeon’s inheritance consisted of cities inside Judah because Judah’s original allotment proved larger than the tribe required (Joshua 19:9). The arrangement displayed Israel’s unity under God’s distribution of the land.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:80: Bethul, meaning “house of God,” was another Simeonite town in the Negev, a dry region suited to herding and seasonal movement. Its identification is uncertain, though it may be connected with Bethuel or Chesil in parallel lists (Joshua 15:30; 1 Chronicles 4:30). Even small and now-unidentified towns stood within the covenant inheritance God gave His people.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:82: 1Ch 4:30 Bethuel, Hormah, Ziklag,
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:93: Jdg 1:17 Then the men of Judah went with their brothers…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:95: Joshua 19:5
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:97: Ziklag,
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:98: These southern towns belonged to Simeon’s inheritance within Judah’s larger territory. Ziklag later became David’s refuge while fleeing Saul, granted to him by Achish of Gath. From Ziklag, David learned of Saul’s death, and there he sought the Lord after the Amalekites raided the city and carried away the families of David and his men.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:100: 1Ch 4:30 Bethuel, Hormah, Ziklag,
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:102: Jos 15:31 Ziklag, Madmannah, Sansannah,
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:104: 1Sa 27:6 That day Achish gave him Ziklag, and to this day…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:107: Beth-marcaboth, meaning “house of chariots,” suggests a place associated with military strength or chariotry. This name indicates the possible presence of a chariot station or a place where chariots were kept or manufactured. The mention of chariots is significant in the biblical context, as they were a symbol of military power and might, often associated with the Canaanites and later the Egyptians. The Israelites’ encounters with chariots, such as in the battle against Sisera (Judges 4:3), highlight the challenges they faced in conquering the  [...]
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:114: Hazar-susah, meaning “village of horses,” indicates a settlement associated with horse breeding or stabling. Horses were valuable assets in ancient warfare and transportation, and their presence in a settlement would signify wealth and strategic importance. The mention of horses connects to the broader biblical narrative where horses and chariots are often seen as symbols of earthly power, which the Israelites were cautioned against relying on instead of trusting in God (Psalm 20:7). The location of Hazar-susah is not definitively known, but it [...]
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:116: 1Ch 4:30-33 Bethuel, Hormah, Ziklag,…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:120: Joshua 19:6
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:123: Beth-lebaoth, “house of lions,” stood among Simeon’s southern settlements within Judah’s larger inheritance. Its name suggests a place of strength or protection, possibly indicating a fortified settlement. God provided Simeon with inhabited places and pasturelands even though the tribe received no separate territory outside Judah.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:127: 1Ch 4:30 Bethuel, Hormah, Ziklag,
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:132: Sharuhen occupied a strategic position in the Negev approaches toward Egypt and had a history as a fortified stronghold. Egyptian records associate the city with major military activity during the early New Kingdom, confirming the importance of controlling this southern corridor. Its inclusion in Simeon’s inheritance placed Israel near a vital boundary region requiring vigilance and faithful stewardship.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:141: The recorded number preserves the ordered distribution of the land among Israel’s tribes. Differences between the stated total and the names in the list may reflect alternate names, associated settlements, or textual transmission, yet the allotment itself is presented as a real and defined inheritance granted by the Lord.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:150: The principal towns depended upon surrounding villages, fields, wells, flocks, and grazing lands. Simeon’s inheritance supplied the setting for ordinary covenant life: families raising children, tending herds, cultivating the land, and worshiping the God who had brought Israel into the promised land.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:152: Jos 13:23 And the border of the Reubenites was the bank of the Jordan…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:154: Jos 13:28 This was the inheritance of the clans of the Gadites,…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:158: Joshua 19:7
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:161: Ain, meaning “spring” or “fountain,” was one of Simeon’s towns within the larger territory of Judah. Its association with a spring points to the importance of dependable water in the southern land, where settlements and flocks depended on such sources.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:168: Rimmon, “pomegranate,” was likely joined with Ain in later references as En-rimmon, “spring of Rimmon.” The town remained inhabited after the exile, when people of Judah resettled in the Negev region (Nehemiah 11:29).
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:172: Neh 11:29 in En-rimmon, Zorah, Jarmuth,
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:175: Ether belonged to Simeon’s inheritance in the southern borderlands. The name Ether means abundance or plenty. Though little else is recorded of the town, its inclusion shows that God’s provision for Simeon extended to smaller communities as well as prominent cities.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:182: Ashan, meaning smoke or cloud, appears in related tribal and Levitical lists, reflecting the close connection between Simeon’s territory and Judah’s land. Levitical cities among these settlements ensured that worship, instruction in God’s law, and priestly ministry remained accessible throughout Israel.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:184: Jos 15:33-47 These were in the foothills: Eshtaol, Zorah, Ashnah,…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:189: The listed towns served as centers for surrounding villages, fields, families, and herds. Simeon received its inheritance within Judah because Judah’s original allotment was larger than needed, demonstrating God’s orderly care for every tribe.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:193: Num 35:2 Command the Israelites to give, from the inheritance…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:197: Joshua 19:8
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:200: Simeon’s inheritance included the smaller settlements dependent on the principal towns. These villages supported the region’s agricultural and pastoral life and ensured that the clans possessed the land and resources needed to dwell securely.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:207: Baalath-beer (“Lord of the well”) marked the southern reach of Simeon’s territory in the Negev, a dry region where access to water was essential. Its inclusion shows that the allotment extended to the boundary settlements as well as the more populated towns.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:213: This was the inheritance of the clans of the tribe of Simeon.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:214: Simeon received its cities within Judah’s larger territory because Judah’s allotment proved more extensive than needed (Joshua 19:9). The arrangement fulfilled Israel’s division of the promised land while also reflecting Jacob’s prophecy that Simeon would be scattered in Israel (Genesis 49:5–7).
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:218: Num 26:12-14 These were the descendants of Simeon by their clans:…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:220: Gen 49:5-7 Simeon and Levi are brothers; their swords are weapons…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:222: Joshua 19:9
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:224: The inheritance of the Simeonites was taken from the territory of Judah,
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:225: Simeon received no separate block of land alongside the other tribes but cities and surrounding pasturelands within Judah’s southern allotment. Jacob had foretold that Simeon would be scattered in Israel because of the violence of Simeon and Levi at Shechem (Genesis 49:5–7). Levi’s scattering became a sacred service among the tribes, while Simeon’s inheritance within Judah reflected diminished tribal influence. Yet the Lord did not abandon Simeon; He gave the tribe a recognized place in the Promised Land.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:227: Jdg 1:3 Then the men of Judah said to their brothers the Simeonites…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:229: Jos 15:1-12 Now the allotment for the clans of the tribe of Judah…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:231: because the share for Judah’s descendants was too large for them.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:232: Judah’s territory extended broadly across southern Canaan, from the wilderness near the Dead Sea to the Mediterranean coast. Its size corresponded to Judah’s population and its leading role among Israel, but the allotment exceeded the tribe’s immediate need. The distribution of part of Judah’s inheritance to Simeon displayed God’s wise provision and Israel’s responsibility to share covenant blessings rather than preserve excess for itself.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:234: Num 26:22 These were the clans of Judah, and their registration…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:238: So the Simeonites received an inheritance within Judah’s portion.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:239: Simeon’s towns were concentrated in the Negev and southern region of Judah (Joshua 19:2–8). The tribe retained its genealogy and identity, though it became closely associated with Judah and later appears among Judah’s cities and settlements (1 Chronicles 4:27–43). This arrangement strengthened the connection between the tribes and anticipated Judah’s later prominence under David. God’s faithfulness was evident even in Simeon’s reduced position: every tribe received its appointed inheritance according to His promise.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:243: Jdg 1:17 Then the men of Judah went with their brothers…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:247: Zebulun’s Inheritance
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:249: Joshua 19:10
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:251: The third lot came up for the clans of the tribe of Zebulun:
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:252: The land was distributed before the Lord by lot, so Zebulun’s inheritance was received as God’s appointed portion rather than gained by human preference or power (Numbers 26:55–56; Joshua 18:6–10). Zebulun, Leah’s sixth son, received territory in northern Canaan among the tribes that would later be associated with Galilee.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:254: Jos 18:10 And Joshua cast lots for them in the presence of the LORD…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:256: Num 26:55-56 Indeed, the land must be divided by lot;…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:260: The border of their inheritance stretched as far as Sarid.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:261: Sarid marked part of Zebulun’s southern boundary, though its precise location is no longer certain. Zebulun’s territory lay near productive valleys and important travel routes, providing opportunities consistent with Jacob’s blessing concerning commerce and access toward the coast (Genesis 49:13). In later generations, the region of Zebulun became closely connected with Galilee, where Jesus was raised and began His public ministry. Isaiah’s promise that light would shine upon the land of Zebulun found its fullest fulfillment in Christ’s ministr [...]
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:263: Gen 49:13 Zebulun shall dwell by the seashore and become a harbor…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:265: Deu 33:18-19 Concerning Zebulun he said: “Rejoice, Zebulun,…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:269: Joshua 19:11
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:272: Zebulun’s boundary advanced toward the Mediterranean region, marking the inheritance God assigned to the tribe. Maralah is otherwise unknown, yet its inclusion preserves the precision of the land God gave His people. Even lesser-known places stood within the faithful fulfillment of His covenant.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:281: Dabbesheth, meaning “hump” or “hill,” served as a recognizable boundary point, probably associated with a hill or raised feature in the landscape. Israel’s inheritance was not an abstract promise but a defined possession, apportioned among the tribes under God’s direction.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:283: Jos 21:28 From the tribe of Issachar they were given Kishion,…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:285: 1Ch 6:72 From the tribe of Issachar they were given Kedesh, Daberath,
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:287: and met the brook east of Jokneam.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:288: The brook formed a natural border near Jokneam, a city associated with the defeated Canaanite kings and later given to the Levites (Joshua 12:22; 21:34). The boundary therefore recalled both Israel’s victory through God’s power and the provision made for worship and instruction within the land.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:296: Joshua 19:12
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:299: Sarid served as a boundary point within Zebulun’s inheritance in Lower Galilee, a fertile and strategically important region.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:301: Deu 33:18 Concerning Zebulun he said: “Rejoice, Zebulun,…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:303: Gen 49:13 Zebulun shall dwell by the seashore and become a harbor…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:305: Jdg 1:30 Zebulun failed to drive out the inhabitants of Kitron…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:308: The carefully marked boundary shows that Israel’s tribal inheritance came by God’s appointment, fulfilling His promise to give the land to Abraham’s descendants.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:316: along the border of Chisloth-tabor
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:317: Chisloth-tabor lay near Mount Tabor, later associated with Deborah and Barak’s victory over Sisera’s army. God’s past deliverance in the region testified that the land belonged to Him before it was entrusted to Zebulun.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:319: Jdg 4:6 She summoned Barak son of Abinoam from Kedesh in Naphtali…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:328: Jos 21:28 From the tribe of Issachar they were given Kishion,…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:330: 1Ch 6:72 From the tribe of Issachar they were given Kedesh, Daberath,
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:333: Japhia was likely near Nazareth in the hill country of Galilee. Zebulun’s inheritance therefore included territory connected with the region where Jesus would later live and begin His public ministry.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:341: Joshua 19:13
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:344: The boundary of Zebulun continued toward the rising sun, marking the inheritance assigned by the Lord through Joshua. Every measured line testified that Israel’s settlement in Canaan rested on God’s covenant faithfulness, not merely military conquest.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:348: Jos 18:12 On the north side their border began at the Jordan,…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:353: Gath-hepher later became known as the hometown of Jonah, the prophet whom God sent to Nineveh (2 Kings 14:25; Jonah 1:1). Jonah’s ministry connected this small town in Zebulun with God’s concern for the nations beyond Israel.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:362: Eth-kazin stood among the towns that defined Zebulun’s allotted territory. Its preservation in the boundary record shows the care with which Israel’s inheritance was established, leaving no tribe without a defined portion in the land God had promised.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:364: Deu 33:18-19 Concerning Zebulun he said: “Rejoice, Zebulun,…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:366: Jdg 5:18 Zebulun was a people who risked their lives…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:371: Rimmon marked another point along Zebulun’s border, likely in the hill country of Lower Galilee. The land’s boundaries gave the tribe a secure place among its brethren and provided the setting for its families, towns, worship, and service in Israel.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:380: The changing course of the boundary reflects an actual, carefully determined division of the land. God gave each tribe its inheritance according to His order, preserving both the unity of Israel and the distinct stewardship entrusted to every tribe.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:388: Joshua 19:14
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:391: Neah served as a recognized landmark on Zebulun’s northern boundary. Though its precise location is now uncertain, its inclusion reflects an actual territorial survey carried out when Joshua distributed the land at Shiloh. Israel’s inheritance was assigned by God, yet its borders were carefully established for orderly possession and peaceful coexistence among the tribes.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:398: Hannathon marked another point along Zebulun’s northern frontier. The name Hannathon means “gracious” or “favored.” The town appears to have been an established settlement in Lower Galilee and is likely reflected in ancient Near Eastern geographical records. Such named locations anchored Zebulun’s inheritance in the real landscape God had promised to give Israel.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:404: Jdg 1:30 Zebulun failed to drive out the inhabitants of Kitron…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:407: The Valley of Iphtah-el, meaning “God opens,” formed part of the boundary between Zebulun and the neighboring tribal lands, including Asher. Its designation as the boundary’s endpoint shows that each tribe received a defined inheritance under God’s covenant faithfulness. The detailed boundaries testify that the Lord’s promise of land was not symbolic alone, but fulfilled in identifiable places for His people.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:413: Joshua 19:15
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:416: Kattath belonged to Zebulun’s inheritance in the fertile region of Lower Galilee. Though its precise location is no longer known, its inclusion shows that God’s allotment embraced smaller settlements as well as prominent towns. Every boundary and city testified that the land was received as a covenant gift, not merely gained by Israel’s strength.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:420: Deu 33:18-19 Concerning Zebulun he said: “Rejoice, Zebulun,…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:423: Nahalal later became one of the cities assigned to the Levites (Joshua 21:35). God placed Levites among the tribes so that worship, instruction in His law, and the ministry of the sanctuary would remain woven into Israel’s daily life. Zebulun’s inheritance therefore included provision for those set apart to serve the Lord.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:425: Jdg 1:30 Zebulun failed to drive out the inhabitants of Kitron…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:431: Shimron,
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:432: Shimron had been ruled by a Canaanite king whose forces joined the northern coalition defeated by Joshua (Joshua 11:1; 12:20). Its presence within Zebulun’s territory marked the fulfillment of God’s promise to dispossess the Canaanite nations and give their land to Israel. The city stood as evidence that the Lord had gone before His people in battle.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:434: Jos 11:1 Now when Jabin king of Hazor heard about these things…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:436: Jos 12:20 the king of Shimron-meron, one; the king of Achshaph, one;
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:439: Idalah formed part of the detailed record of Zebulun’s possession. Such careful identification of cities preserved the tribe’s rightful inheritance and demonstrated the orderliness of God’s provision. Israel’s place in the land rested upon the Lord’s faithfulness to His covenant with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:441: Gen 49:13 Zebulun shall dwell by the seashore and become a harbor…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:448: Bethlehem of Zebulun was a Galilean town, distinct from Bethlehem of Judah, the city of David and the birthplace of the Messiah. The shared name reflects the ordinary use of place names throughout Israel, while the distinction preserves the unique biblical importance of Bethlehem in Judah.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:457: The cities and their surrounding villages made up a settled inheritance where Zebulun’s families could live, cultivate the land, raise their children, and worship the Lord. The complete record confirms that God fulfilled His promise to establish Israel in the land He had pledged to their fathers.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:459: Jos 21:40 In all, twelve cities were allotted to the clans of Merari,…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:465: Joshua 19:16
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:467: This was the inheritance of the clans of the tribe of Zebulun,
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:468: Zebulun received its allotted territory in the lower Galilee region, surrounded by the lands of several fellow tribes. The inheritance confirmed God’s faithfulness to His covenant promise that Abraham’s descendants would possess Canaan. As Jacob’s sixth son by Leah, Zebulun became the father of a distinct tribal community whose land placed it near important routes connecting the Mediterranean coast, the Jezreel Valley, and the Sea of Galilee. Isaiah later identified this region with “the land of Zebulun,” and Matthew saw its association with Ga [...]
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:470: Deu 33:18-19 Concerning Zebulun he said: “Rejoice, Zebulun,…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:472: Gen 49:13 Zebulun shall dwell by the seashore and become a harbor…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:477: The towns and surrounding settlements gave Zebulun’s clans places for homes, agriculture, worship, defense, and trade. Israel’s inheritance was not merely a geographic possession but a covenant gift requiring faithful stewardship under the Lord. Each village belonged within the ordered life of the tribe, reminding Israel that the land, its produce, and its security ultimately came from God.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:485: Issachar’s Inheritance
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:487: Joshua 19:17
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:489: The fourth lot came out
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:490: The allotment was determined before the LORD at Shiloh through Eleazar the priest, Joshua, and Israel’s tribal leaders. Issachar received the fourth inheritance, following Judah, Simeon, and Zebulun. The lot upheld God’s sovereign direction over the land He had promised to Abraham’s descendants, for “the decision is from the LORD” (Proverbs 16:33).
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:492: Num 26:55-56 Indeed, the land must be divided by lot;…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:494: Jos 18:10 And Joshua cast lots for them in the presence of the LORD…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:496: Pro 16:33 The lot is cast into the lap, but its every decision…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:499: Issachar’s inheritance was assigned among its family divisions, preserving each household’s place within the covenant community. Land was not merely economic property; it was a God-given inheritance tied to family identity, worship, responsibility, and future generations.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:501: Num 33:54 And you are to divide the land by lot according to your clans…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:505: of the tribe of Issachar:
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:506: Issachar, Leah’s fifth son, received territory in and around the fertile Jezreel Valley and Lower Galilee. Its productive fields and strategic location contributed to the tribe’s later importance in Israel’s history. Men of Issachar were recognized for understanding the times and knowing what Israel should do (1 Chronicles 12:32), demonstrating that God’s gifts to His people included both material provision and wisdom for faithful service.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:508: Gen 49:14-15 Issachar is a strong donkey, lying down between sheepfolds…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:510: Num 26:23-25 These were the descendants of Issachar by their clans:…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:512: Joshua 19:18
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:515: Jezreel, meaning “God sows,” stood at the eastern end of the fertile valley that bore its name, within Issachar’s inheritance. Its commanding location overlooked major routes through northern Israel and later became associated with Ahab’s royal residence, Naboth’s faithful refusal to surrender his vineyard, and God’s judgment upon Ahab’s house. Hosea also used Jezreel to portray both divine scattering in judgment and future restoration under God’s covenant mercy.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:523: Chesulloth,
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:524: Chesulloth, meaning “loins” or “flanks,” lay in the hill country bordering the Jezreel Valley, near the approaches to Mount Tabor. Its inclusion among Issachar’s towns reflects the tribe’s possession of productive farmland and strategic highlands in a region repeatedly affected by Israel’s wars and deliverance.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:526: Jdg 4:6-14 She summoned Barak son of Abinoam from Kedesh in Naphtali…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:541: Joshua 19:19
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:544: Hapharaim, meaning “two pits” or “double pit,” belonged to the network of towns granted to Issachar in the fertile heartland of northern Israel. Its inclusion testifies to the concrete fulfillment of God’s promise to give Israel an inheritance in the land.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:546: Deu 33:23 Concerning Naphtali he said: “Naphtali is abounding with…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:550: Mat 4:15 Land of Zebulun and land of Naphtali, the Way of the Sea…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:553: Shion, meaning “ruin” or “desolation,” stood among the communities through which Issachar’s families were settled and sustained. Even places otherwise unknown in later history remained known to God and formed part of Israel’s covenant inheritance.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:555: Jdg 4:6 She summoned Barak son of Abinoam from Kedesh in Naphtali…
