C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:599: Joshua 19:21
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:602: Remeth is a town located in the territory allotted to the tribe of Issachar. The name “Remeth” means “height” or “elevation,” suggesting its geographical positioning. This town is also referred to as “Jarmuth” in some biblical texts, indicating a possible dual name or a change over time. The location of Remeth is not precisely identified today, but it is believed to have been situated in the fertile Jezreel Valley, an area known for its agricultural productivity. This region’s fertility would have been significant for the tribe of Issachar, kno [...]
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:606: 1Ch 6:72 From the tribe of Issachar they were given Kedesh, Daberath,
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:608: Jos 21:28 From the tribe of Issachar they were given Kishion,…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:611: En-gannim, meaning “spring of gardens,” indicates a place with abundant water sources, making it ideal for cultivation and settlement. This town is identified with the modern-day Jenin, located at the northern edge of the West Bank. En-gannim’s strategic location at the crossroads of major trade routes would have made it an important site for commerce and interaction with neighboring regions. The presence of gardens and springs aligns with the biblical imagery of a land flowing with milk and honey, symbolizing God’s provision and blessing.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:615: Jos 17:11 Within Issachar and Asher, Manasseh was assigned Beth-shean,…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:629: Its precise site is unknown, but Beth-pazzez, meaning “house of dispersion,” completed part of the inheritance entrusted to Issachar. The detailed record of these towns shows that the Promised Land was not an abstract blessing but a real inheritance of fields, homes, springs, and communities, given by the Lord to His covenant people.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:637: Joshua 19:22
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:640: Mount Tabor stood prominently in lower Galilee and served as a recognizable boundary marker for Issachar’s inheritance. Its height and central location made it strategically important in later Israelite history. Christian tradition associates Mount Tabor with the Transfiguration of Jesus, though the Gospels do not name the mountain.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:642: Jdg 4:6 She summoned Barak son of Abinoam from Kedesh in Naphtali…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:649: Shahazumah is known only from this boundary description. Its inclusion shows that God’s allotment of the land was defined with care, extending even to lesser-known settlements and landmarks.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:651: Jos 21:28 From the tribe of Issachar they were given Kishion,…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:653: 1Ch 6:72 From the tribe of Issachar they were given Kedesh, Daberath,
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:658: Beth-shemesh, “house of the sun,” was located within Issachar’s territory and should not be confused with the Beth-shemesh in Judah where the Philistines returned the ark of the covenant. The name may preserve evidence of earlier Canaanite sun worship, yet the land belonged to the Lord and was given to Israel for faithful covenant life.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:666: and ended at the Jordan.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:667: The Jordan formed Issachar’s eastern boundary. The river recalled Israel’s entrance into the Promised Land under Joshua and testified that the inheritance had been received by God’s power, not by Israel’s strength.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:669: Num 34:12 Then the border will go down along the Jordan…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:671: Deu 3:17 The Jordan River in the Arabah bordered it…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:673: Jos 13:23 And the border of the Reubenites was the bank of the Jordan…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:676: Issachar received established towns together with the surrounding villages and agricultural lands needed to sustain its people. The detailed division of the land confirms the Lord’s faithfulness to His promise to give Abraham’s descendants the land of Canaan.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:684: Joshua 19:23
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:686: This was the inheritance of the clans of the tribe of Issachar,
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:687: Issachar received its allotted portion in the fertile Jezreel Valley and surrounding regions, an area well suited for grain, vineyards, and livestock. The land fulfilled God’s covenant promise to Abraham and gave each clan within Issachar a lasting place among the tribes of Israel. Jacob had portrayed Issachar as a strong donkey content to bear burdens in a good land (Genesis 49:14–15), and the tribe’s inheritance reflects both agricultural abundance and the labor required to cultivate and defend it. The division by clans preserved family ident [...]
