C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:753: Achshaph,
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:754: Achshaph had been a royal Canaanite city whose king joined Jabin’s northern coalition against Joshua (Joshua 11:1). The Lord gave victory over that coalition, and Achshaph became part of the land entrusted to Asher, testifying that Israel’s inheritance rested upon God’s faithfulness and power.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:756: Jos 11:1 Now when Jabin king of Hazor heard about these things…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:758: Jos 12:20 the king of Shimron-meron, one; the king of Achshaph, one;
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:760: Joshua 19:26
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:763: These towns formed part of Asher’s allotted inheritance in northern Israel. Though their locations are no longer certain and they receive no further biblical attention, their inclusion records the careful fulfillment of the Lord’s promise to give each tribe its appointed possession. God’s faithfulness extended to villages as well as to prominent cities, establishing Asher within the land promised to Israel.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:765: Jos 21:30 From the tribe of Asher they were given Mishal, Abdon,
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:767: 1Ch 6:74 From the tribe of Asher they were given Mashal, Abdon,
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:769: Jdg 1:31 Asher failed to drive out the inhabitants of Acco, Sidon,…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:771: On the west the border touched Carmel
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:772: Mount Carmel marked an important boundary near the Mediterranean coast, joining fertile agricultural lands with a strategic ridge overlooking major travel routes. Centuries later, Carmel became the setting for Elijah’s confrontation with the prophets of Baal, where the Lord demonstrated His authority over the false gods associated with fertility and rain (1 Kings 18). The boundary of Asher thus included territory whose richness testified to the Lord’s provision.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:774: 1Ki 18:19 Now summon all Israel to meet me on Mount Carmel,…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:778: 2Ki 4:25 So she set out and went to the man of God at Mount Carmel…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:781: Shihor-libnath—whose name suggests a water source, with Shihor meaning “black” or “dark” and Libnath possibly meaning “whiteness”—was likely a stream, coastal landmark, or settlement near Carmel that helped define Asher’s western border. Its exact location has not been securely identified, yet it served a definite purpose in Israel’s inheritance. The detailed boundaries preserved in Joshua show that the land was not seized at random but distributed under God’s direction among the tribes.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:789: Joshua 19:27
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:792: Beth-dagon, meaning “house of Dagon,” marked a place associated with Dagon, a chief Philistine god. Its presence along Asher’s boundary shows that Israel’s inheritance lay beside peoples devoted to false worship. God gave Israel defined land and a distinct calling, yet the tribes would continually face the temptation to accommodate the religions and practices of their neighbors.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:798: touched Zebulun and the Valley of Iphtah-el,
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:799: Asher’s border met the territory of Zebulun, demonstrating that each tribal inheritance formed part of one unified covenant land. The Valley of Iphtah-el provided a recognizable natural landmark for the boundary. God’s distribution of the land was neither vague nor accidental; each tribe received an appointed place within Israel.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:801: Deu 33:18-19 Concerning Zebulun he said: “Rejoice, Zebulun,…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:803: Gen 49:13 Zebulun shall dwell by the seashore and become a harbor…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:806: The northward course identified further settlements that secured Asher’s allotted territory. Such details preserved the tribe’s inheritance and affirmed the faithfulness of God in bringing Israel into the land promised to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob. The land was a gift to be possessed in obedience, not merely a possession to be enjoyed.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:813: Cabul, meaning “bound” or “displeasing,” lay in the region later associated with Solomon’s dealings with Hiram of Tyre, when Solomon gave Hiram towns in Galilee as payment for materials and labor used in building the temple and royal projects (1 Kings 9:10–13). The proximity of Tyre and other Phoenician centers gave Asher opportunities for trade and influence, but also exposed Israel to foreign alliances and idolatry.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:819: Joshua 19:28
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:822: Ebron marked part of Asher’s northern inheritance in the land promised to Israel. The detailed boundary record affirms that each tribe received an appointed portion under God’s covenant provision.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:824: Jos 21:30 From the tribe of Asher they were given Mishal, Abdon,
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:826: 1Ch 6:74 From the tribe of Asher they were given Mashal, Abdon,
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:829: Rehob belonged within Asher’s allotted territory, yet Asher later failed to drive out its Canaanite inhabitants (Judges 1:31). Israel’s incomplete obedience left pagan populations in the land and exposed later generations to spiritual compromise.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:833: Jdg 1:31 Asher failed to drive out the inhabitants of Acco, Sidon,…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:836: Hammon formed another point along Asher’s boundary. Its inclusion preserves the historical reality of Israel’s settlement and shows the care taken in establishing each tribe’s inheritance.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:838: 1Ch 6:76 And from the tribe of Naphtali they were given Kedesh…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:840: Jos 21:32 And from the tribe of Naphtali they were given…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:843: Kanah, meaning “reed” or “place of reeds,” helped define Asher’s border near the northern reaches of the land. God’s promises were not vague grants but were fulfilled through real places, boundaries, and tribal possessions.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:850: Greater Sidon was a powerful Phoenician city north of Israel’s territory. Asher’s proximity to Sidon placed the tribe beside a wealthy maritime culture marked by idolatry, creating both economic contact and continuing pressure to remain faithful to the Lord.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:858: Joshua 19:29
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:861: Ramah marked another change in the northern boundary of Asher and should not be confused with other biblical towns bearing the same name. The careful record of each turn shows that Asher’s inheritance was a real and defined gift from the Lord, not a vague claim upon the land.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:863: 1Ki 15:17 Baasha king of Israel went to war against Judah…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:870: Tyre was a powerful Phoenician city, renowned for its defenses, trade, and seafaring influence. Its place on Asher’s boundary brought Israel into close contact with a wealthy Canaanite culture. Asher later failed to drive out all the inhabitants of its territory, and the tribe lived among the Canaanites of the region (Judges 1:31–32).
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:879: Hosah marked the boundary’s movement toward the coast, though its precise location is no longer known. The inclusion of such places preserves the historical reality of Israel’s tribal allotments and the faithfulness of God in giving each tribe its appointed inheritance.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:888: The Mediterranean Sea formed Asher’s western boundary near Achzib, a coastal settlement north of Acco. Access to the sea offered opportunities for travel and commerce, yet it also placed Asher beside Phoenician cities whose prosperity and idolatry would test Israel’s covenant faithfulness.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:890: Jdg 1:31 Asher failed to drive out the inhabitants of Acco, Sidon,…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:892: Jdg 5:17 Gilead remained beyond the Jordan.…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:896: Joshua 19:30
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:899: Ummah belonged to the inheritance assigned to Asher in the northern land of Canaan. Its precise location is uncertain, yet its inclusion in the boundary record confirms that God’s promise of a settled inheritance extended to communities both prominent and obscure.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:901: Deu 33:24-25 And concerning Asher he said: “May Asher be the most favored…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:903: Gen 49:20 Asher’s food will be rich; he shall provide royal delicacies…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:906: Aphek was likely a fortified site within Asher’s territory, though its exact identification remains debated. Other places named Aphek appear in accounts of Israel’s conflicts with the Philistines and Syrians, reminding readers that the land of promise still required faith, obedience, and perseverance amid hostile powers.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:915: Rehob was another Asherite city, possibly connected with Beth-rehob in the far north near the approaches to Lebanon. Its name means “broad” or “spacious.” Its place in Asher’s inheritance reflects the breadth of the territory God gave His people, including fertile valleys, coastal routes, and strategic northern settlements.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:919: Jdg 1:31 Asher failed to drive out the inhabitants of Acco, Sidon,…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:921: 1Ch 6:74-75 From the tribe of Asher they were given Mashal, Abdon,…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:924: The detailed count records a real and ordered distribution of the land among Israel’s tribes. God did not merely promise Canaan in general terms; He apportioned cities, villages, fields, and boundaries for the life of His covenant people. Asher’s inheritance testified to the Lord’s faithfulness, even though later generations would be responsible to live faithfully within what He had given.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:930: Joshua 19:31
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:932: This was the inheritance of the clans of the tribe of Asher,
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:933: Asher, meaning “happy” or “blessed,” received its allotted territory in the northern part of Canaan, extending along the Mediterranean coast and into fertile inland regions. The inheritance was assigned by lot under Joshua’s leadership, showing that Israel’s settlement in the land rested on the Lord’s covenant faithfulness rather than human ambition. Jacob had foretold abundance for Asher, and Moses later blessed the tribe with rich provisions and oil (Genesis 49:20; Deuteronomy 33:24–25). Its coastal location gave Asher access to productive fa [...]
