C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:963: for the clans of the tribe of Naphtali:
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:964: Naphtali, descended from Jacob’s sixth son by Bilhah, received territory in northern Canaan, including much of Galilee and areas near the Sea of Galilee. Its fertile valleys and major trade routes made the region strategically important and frequently vulnerable to invasion. Centuries later, the land of Naphtali became associated with the promised light of Isaiah 9:1–2, fulfilled when Jesus began His public ministry in Galilee (Matthew 4:13–16).
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:966: Num 26:48-50 These were the descendants of Naphtali by their clans:…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:968: Deu 33:23 Concerning Naphtali he said: “Naphtali is abounding with…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:970: Gen 49:21 Naphtali is a doe set free that bears beautiful fawns.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:972: Joshua 19:33
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:975: Heleph marked the beginning of Naphtali’s allotted inheritance in the land promised by God to Abraham’s descendants. Although its precise location is uncertain, the boundary record testifies that Naphtali received a real and defined portion within the covenant land.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:984: The great tree served as a recognized landmark in a landscape where prominent natural features helped preserve tribal boundaries. Zaanannim later appears near Kedesh, where Heber the Kenite camped during the days of Deborah and Barak (Judges 4:11), linking Naphtali’s territory with the conflict in which the Lord delivered Israel from Sisera.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:990: Jos 24:26 Joshua recorded these things in the Book of the Law of God.…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:993: Adami-nekeb and Jabneel marked successive points along Naphtali’s border. Their inclusion shows the care taken to protect each tribe’s inheritance and to prevent confusion over the land entrusted to them by the Lord.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:997: Jdg 1:31 Asher failed to drive out the inhabitants of Acco, Sidon,…
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:1010: and ending at the Jordan.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:1011: The Jordan River completed the eastern limit of Naphtali’s territory. Israel had crossed that river under Joshua’s leadership to enter the Promised Land, and it continued to stand as a lasting reminder that the tribes lived in an inheritance received by God’s grace and governed by His covenant.
