C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:1058: Judah’s principal inheritance lay far south of Naphtali, making this boundary reference difficult to identify geographically. The Jordan nevertheless stood as Israel’s eastern frontier and as a lasting witness to the Lord’s power in bringing His people into their inheritance.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:1066: Joshua 19:35
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:1069: Naphtali’s inheritance included strongholds that guarded the northern approaches to Israel and helped secure the tribe’s settled life in the land God had given. Walls, gates, and towers provided defense in times of invasion while also serving the ordinary needs of government, trade, and refuge.
C:\Obsidian\Hermes\scripture\raw_data\biblehub_study_joshua_19.txt:1078: Ziddim stood within Naphtali’s network of defended settlements, though its precise location is no longer known. Its preservation in the territorial record testifies to the completeness of God’s provision for every part of the tribe’s inheritance.
